---
/**
 * P1.2 - Milligram math from the label.
 * P1.3 - Pipette markings: precision dosing without guesswork.
 * Interactive apothecary pipette visual synchronized with dose calculator.
 */
import InteractiveDropperMath from './InteractiveDropperMath';
---

<section id="dose" class="w-full bg-surface-container-low border-y border-outline-variant/30">
  <div class="max-w-container-max mx-auto px-margin-mobile md:px-margin-desktop py-20 md:py-28">
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-stack-md reveal mb-12">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 bg-secondary-container/60 rounded-full border border-secondary/30">
        <span class="w-1.5 h-1.5 rounded-full bg-secondary" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="font-label-sm text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-secondary">
          Visual Reference Method
        </span>
      </div>
      <h2 class="font-headline-lg text-headline-lg text-primary">See the draw before you take it.</h2>
      <p class="font-body-lg text-body-lg text-on-surface-variant leading-relaxed">
        The current dropper is unmarked. These visual references make it easier to recognize a similar fill level later.
      </p>
      <p class="font-body-sm text-body-sm text-on-surface-variant italic">
        Visual levels are approximate. They are not calibrated volume markings.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <InteractiveDropperMath client:visible />
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-outline-variant/30 text-on-surface-variant font-body-md text-sm">
      <p class="leading-relaxed">
        Observing visible fill height before administration allows you to recognize a comparable draw across different evenings. Maintaining a consistent visual reference helps you build an understanding of your routine.
      </p>
      <p class="leading-relaxed">
        Because the glass dropper is unmarked, reference states provide a helpful visual guide rather than a laboratory measurement. Notice the level against the glass, keep it steady, and observe the experience.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

